WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.180 --> 00:00:08.799
Everyone seems to know everything about everything
these days.

00:00:08.799 --> 00:00:14.020
I don't mean people seem to just have an opinion
on any given topic which is great, and that's

00:00:14.020 --> 00:00:15.300
what free speech is all about.

00:00:15.300 --> 00:00:19.859
Instead, what I'm talking about here is the
constant and endless stream of people pretending

00:00:19.859 --> 00:00:24.640
that they know exactly how to run the world
if only everyone would listen to them.

00:00:24.640 --> 00:00:25.890
Iran nuclear deal?

00:00:25.890 --> 00:00:29.320
The anime Twitter fox knows all about enriched
uranium.

00:00:29.320 --> 00:00:31.150
How to destroy ISIS?

00:00:31.150 --> 00:00:34.789
You're unemployed cousin knows exactly demolish
them!

00:00:34.789 --> 00:00:36.560
The best way to administer healthcare?

00:00:36.560 --> 00:00:40.841
Your mom's friend on Facebook who can't figure
out how to unlock her iPad has figured it

00:00:40.841 --> 00:00:43.760
out how to make single payer work!

00:00:43.760 --> 00:00:48.790
This constant constant expression of fake
knowledge is partly a function of social media,

00:00:48.790 --> 00:00:53.100
which rewards us for quick hits and maximum
snark, but it is also a function of something

00:00:53.100 --> 00:00:57.460
that's been built into us since our beginning...the
human ego.

00:00:57.460 --> 00:01:00.660
Ego is constantly rewarded in this crazy world
we live in.

00:01:00.660 --> 00:01:04.420
Do you think the rise of Trump might have
a little something to do with ego?

00:01:04.420 --> 00:01:09.280
Clearly Trump understands the way we all connect
to a powerful personality and he uses that

00:01:09.280 --> 00:01:15.240
power in every interaction that he has be
it via a speech, a meeting or on Twitter.

00:01:15.240 --> 00:01:20.150
But beneath this connection seems to be the
fragile ego of a child who must lash out any

00:01:20.150 --> 00:01:22.790
time they hear anything not to their liking.

00:01:22.790 --> 00:01:27.479
By the way, I don't think a politician using
the persuasive power of personality something

00:01:27.479 --> 00:01:29.640
new as far as the president is concerned.

00:01:29.640 --> 00:01:33.380
While Trump is a particularly interesting
case of ego in the White House, let's not

00:01:33.380 --> 00:01:38.600
pretend Barack Obama with his promises of
hope and change didn't have one as well.

00:01:38.600 --> 00:01:42.790
Or Bill Clinton, who was a master at making
every person in the room feel like they were

00:01:42.790 --> 00:01:45.299
the only person he cared about.

00:01:45.299 --> 00:01:49.770
While there isn't a direct comparison to of
the charm of Obama and Clinton to the alpha

00:01:49.770 --> 00:01:55.220
male tactics of Trump, there is an interesting
through line to what types of people we respond

00:01:55.220 --> 00:01:56.220
to.

00:01:56.220 --> 00:02:01.310
Interestingly, I'm not sure you could say
that George W. Bush falls into this strong

00:02:01.310 --> 00:02:02.640
ego bucket.

00:02:02.640 --> 00:02:08.039
Like W or not, there was a humbleness, perhaps
born of insecurity, he had and I think the

00:02:08.039 --> 00:02:09.259
others lacked.

00:02:09.259 --> 00:02:12.990
Of course W had his own set of problems, but
that's a whole other story.

00:02:12.990 --> 00:02:14.549
Now let me back up for a second.

00:02:14.549 --> 00:02:19.819
I don't think having an ego is bad-- after
all, each of us has an ego in some way.

00:02:19.819 --> 00:02:24.439
By definition, an ego is a person's sense
of self-esteem or self importance.

00:02:24.439 --> 00:02:28.889
Of course having a sense of self-esteem and
self importance is good, overall.

00:02:28.889 --> 00:02:32.379
Nobody wants to be walking around feeling
worthless and irrelevant.

00:02:32.379 --> 00:02:37.650
Feeling worthless isn't healthy for you, and
it's not great for anyone around you, either.

00:02:37.650 --> 00:02:42.579
Of course on the other hand, an inflated sense
importance can be dangerous.

00:02:42.579 --> 00:02:48.300
Here's where I have to be careful --many of
the brightest minds in arts, sports, politics,

00:02:48.300 --> 00:02:54.199
science and virtually every other field have
an inflated sense of self importance, and

00:02:54.199 --> 00:02:58.340
this trait is partly what helps them in achieving
greatness.

00:02:58.340 --> 00:03:03.389
I think it's fair to say the astronomical
success of Steve Jobs, Michael Jordan and

00:03:03.389 --> 00:03:08.769
Beyonce all have a little something to do
with their sense of self importance, driven

00:03:08.769 --> 00:03:12.109
by an inflated self esteem.

00:03:12.109 --> 00:03:17.650
The endless dedication, hard work and sacrifice
it takes to be a leader in your field often

00:03:17.650 --> 00:03:19.310
can be born out of ego.

00:03:19.310 --> 00:03:24.610
To pretend this doesn't exist in people we
love and admire, or that it is exclusively

00:03:24.610 --> 00:03:28.870
a bad thing, would be to deny something very
human.

00:03:28.870 --> 00:03:33.150
And by the way, I won't be presumptuous enough
to think I don't have an ego myself, after

00:03:33.150 --> 00:03:35.400
all, I talk on camera for a living.

00:03:35.400 --> 00:03:39.999
The ego we all have, to various degrees, is
partly what's causing such disconnect in our

00:03:39.999 --> 00:03:41.530
conversations right now.

00:03:41.530 --> 00:03:46.849
We judge too quickly, we jump to conclusions
without evidence and we condemn too fast,

00:03:46.849 --> 00:03:51.510
all somehow guised as a way of showing how
smart and how correct we are.

00:03:51.510 --> 00:03:55.089
I for one have no problem telling you I don't
have all the answers.

00:03:55.089 --> 00:03:58.810
I think you guys know most of my opinions
on the big issues and I love sharing them

00:03:58.810 --> 00:04:02.150
with you as my opinions change, evolve, or
strengthen.

00:04:02.150 --> 00:04:06.659
But on major, complex issues, like the Iran
deal, or Healthcare, or abortion, or gun control,

00:04:06.659 --> 00:04:08.669
I don't pretend to have every answer.

00:04:08.669 --> 00:04:13.799
Do you think most of the so called experts
who pontificate on everything actually read

00:04:13.799 --> 00:04:21.370
the full text of the Iran deal ? How many
experts or even congressmen actually fully

00:04:21.370 --> 00:04:25.229
read Obama care or the Republican replacement?

00:04:25.229 --> 00:04:28.699
I'm guessing some did, but a very small minority.

00:04:28.699 --> 00:04:33.189
Instead of doing the rigorous work to figure
out what is best, they instead pretend to

00:04:33.189 --> 00:04:39.129
know everything, and make us feel as if only
if we would listen to them, we'd have a perfect

00:04:39.129 --> 00:04:40.129
society.

00:04:40.129 --> 00:04:42.520
It's silly, lazy thinking.

00:04:42.520 --> 00:04:46.800
Of course our mainstream news, which doles
out supposed important conversations in 5

00:04:46.800 --> 00:04:51.250
minute clips with 8 different guests all talking
over each other at once really isn't helping

00:04:51.250 --> 00:04:52.919
the situation.

00:04:52.919 --> 00:04:57.729
And on the broader issues like abortion or
gun control, pretending that you know exactly

00:04:57.729 --> 00:05:03.000
how the law should be so that everything will
work out just right for society is equally

00:05:03.000 --> 00:05:04.600
as nonsensical.

00:05:04.600 --> 00:05:10.509
We live in a pluralistic society and the human
factor, the simple fact that we're all individuals,

00:05:10.509 --> 00:05:14.210
is proof that nothing will ever be perfect
for all of us.

00:05:14.210 --> 00:05:18.270
What we can do is limit the amount of control
we have over each other, while at the same

00:05:18.270 --> 00:05:23.710
time respecting others beliefs and the same
right to property and happiness that we want

00:05:23.710 --> 00:05:25.969
others to afford to us.

00:05:25.969 --> 00:05:30.000
Most of us want sensible answers and being
real with ourselves is the only way we're

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:31.069
gonna get there.

00:05:31.069 --> 00:05:36.060
Saying "I don't know" or "I haven't decided"
or "I need more information" shouldn't be

00:05:36.060 --> 00:05:39.199
seen as a weakness, it should be seen as an
asset.

00:05:39.199 --> 00:05:44.310
Oh, and by the way, of course I'm guilty of
sometimes jumping too conclusions and judging

00:05:44.310 --> 00:05:47.069
before I have the proper information.

00:05:47.069 --> 00:05:52.939
And again, I have an ego too, one by the way
which you often help me keep in check...But

00:05:52.939 --> 00:05:57.730
I will never pretend I have all the answers,
and if I ever do I have no doubt you fine

00:05:57.730 --> 00:05:59.320
folks will let me know in the comments right
down below...

